5.0 Results and Discussion
This chapter presents and discusses the results obtained from the practical evaluation of the proposed defence-in-depth architecture. The experiments were conducted using a virtualised enterprise-style network comprising a Windows endpoint, Kali Linux as the controlled testing host, Snort IDS, and pfSense UTM. The evaluation focused on determining how effectively the individual security controls performed their intended functions and how the controls complemented one another when considered as part of a layered security architecture.
The experiments evaluated network visibility, reconnaissance activity, endpoint-level traffic enforcement, intrusion detection, and gateway-level traffic filtering. Rather than treating the security controls as interchangeable, the evaluation considered the distinct security function performed by each control and the point in the network at which that control operates.
The results demonstrate that no individual security mechanism provides complete protection across all traffic conditions. Windows Defender Firewall was effective at enforcing endpoint - level access restrictions, Snort provided network - level visibility and detection through signature - based alerting, while pfSense provided policy - based filtering for traffic traversing the network gateway. Collectively, these results support the principle of defence-in-depth by demonstrating complementary rather than redundant security controls.
5.1 Baseline Results
The first practical evaluation established the baseline connectivity between the security infrastructure and the protected Windows endpoint. ICMP Echo Requests were initially sent from the Snort IDS host to the Windows 11 Professional virtual machine using: 
ping 192.168.1.111
Observation
The initial ICMP requests were unsuccessful because the Windows Defender Firewall was configured to block inbound ICMP Echo Requests. Consequently, the Windows endpoint did not return ICMP Echo Replies to the Snort host.
This initial result demonstrated the effectiveness of the Windows Defender Firewall as an endpoint-level security control. In its default or restrictive configuration, unsolicited inbound traffic can be prevented from reaching the endpoint unless an appropriate firewall policy explicitly permits it.
Resolution
To establish the required communication for the laboratory experiments, an inbound ICMP firewall rule was configured on the Windows 11 endpoint. The rule permitted ICMP traffic originating from the trusted security infrastructure:
•	192.168.1.1 - pfSense UTM gateway 
•	192.168.1.110 - Snort IDS host 
Following implementation of the rule, the ICMP connectivity test was repeated from the Snort host. The Windows endpoint successfully responded to the ICMP Echo Requests, confirming that communication had been established.
The same connectivity test was subsequently performed from the pfSense UTM gateway to the Windows endpoint. The ping was successful, further confirming connectivity between the gateway and the protected endpoint.
Result and Discussion
The baseline experiment successfully established controlled connectivity between the Windows endpoint and the trusted security infrastructure while maintaining restrictions on unauthorised inbound traffic. The results demonstrate that Windows Defender Firewall can be configured to distinguish between trusted and untrusted sources, allowing required security infrastructure communications while restricting unsolicited traffic.
The successful communication between the Snort host, pfSense gateway and Windows endpoint provided a validated baseline for the subsequent experiments. This was particularly important because the later evaluations relied on the ability to distinguish between legitimate trusted traffic and unauthorised traffic generated by the Kali Linux testing host.
Therefore, the baseline experiment established both the operational connectivity of the laboratory environment and the initial effectiveness of the endpoint firewall as a controlled security boundary. It provided the foundation for the subsequent evaluation of network reconnaissance, Windows endpoint protection, Snort IDS detection, and pfSense UTM traffic filtering and blocking.
5.2 Network Reconnaissance Results
The network reconnaissance experiment evaluated the ability of the Kali Linux testing host to identify services exposed by the Windows 11 Professional endpoint. The assessment focused on commonly used Microsoft networking services that may represent potential entry points for further reconnaissance or exploitation within an enterprise environment.
The scan identified several Microsoft networking services associated with the Windows endpoint, including TCP ports 135, 139 and 445. These services support legitimate enterprise functions such as remote procedure calls, file sharing and network resource access; however, their exposure can increase the attack surface if they are not appropriately secured.
Port 135 (MSRPC) is associated with Microsoft's Remote Procedure Call services and can provide information about services operating on a Windows system. Consequently, it is frequently examined during network reconnaissance and may become a target where vulnerable RPC services are exposed.
Port 139 (NetBIOS Session Service) supports legacy Windows networking and file-sharing functionality. Although its use has declined in modern environments, unnecessary exposure of NetBIOS services can provide additional information to an attacker and potentially increase opportunities for unauthorised access.
Port 445 (Microsoft-DS/SMB) is particularly significant in Windows enterprise environments because SMB supports file and printer sharing as well as other Windows networking functions. SMB has historically been associated with significant security incidents, including the WannaCry ransomware outbreak, making TCP/445 an important target during reconnaissance and subsequent exploitation attempts.
The reconnaissance results demonstrate that legitimate enterprise functionality can create an expanded attack surface. Consequently, the presence of these services should be carefully controlled according to organisational requirements rather than simply assumed to be safe because they are legitimate Windows services.
The subsequent firewall evaluation further demonstrated the importance of access control. Although these services may be required for legitimate organisational operations, Windows Defender Firewall can restrict unauthorised access to them. In addition, the deployment of Snort IDS provides a separate detection capability for suspicious network activity, while pfSense provides gateway-level traffic filtering for traffic that traverses the gateway.
Overall, the reconnaissance experiment established the importance of service exposure management as an early component of defence-in-depth. Identifying exposed services provides visibility into the endpoint's attack surface, while combining endpoint firewall enforcement, IDS monitoring and gateway-level filtering provides additional layers of protection against subsequent reconnaissance and exploitation attempts.
5.3 Windows Endpoint Protection Results
The Windows endpoint protection experiment evaluated the effectiveness of Windows Defender Firewall in preventing unauthorised network traffic from the Kali Linux testing host (192.168.1.113) from reaching the Windows 11 Professional endpoint (192.168.1.111). The firewall was configured to permit trusted traffic from the pfSense gateway (192.168.1.1) and Snort IDS host (192.168.1.110), while traffic originating from the Kali testing host was not included within the permitted sources.
Findings
The experiment demonstrated that the Windows Defender Firewall successfully enforced the configured source-based access-control policy. When Kali attempted to communicate directly with the Windows endpoint, the traffic was not permitted to establish the expected communication.
The TCP reconnaissance test provided further evidence of this behaviour. A controlled Nmap SYN scan was performed against ports 135, 139 and 445 on the Windows endpoint:
sudo nmap -Pn -sS -p 135,139,445 192.168.1.111
The scan identified the Windows host as reachable; however, the tested ports were reported as filtered. This indicates that the endpoint firewall was filtering the unsolicited traffic rather than exposing the services directly to the unauthorised Kali host.
The experiment therefore demonstrated that the Windows endpoint could remain connected to the network while simultaneously restricting access to protected services. This is an important security characteristic because completely isolating an endpoint from the network is often impractical in an enterprise environment.
Results
The results can be summarised as follows:
Test	Source	Destination	Result
ICMP communication from unauthorised host	Kali 192.168.1.113	Windows 192.168.1.111	Blocked
TCP/135 reconnaissance	Kali 192.168.1.113	Windows 192.168.1.111	Filtered
TCP/139 reconnaissance	Kali 192.168.1.113	Windows 192.168.1.111	Filtered
TCP/445 reconnaissance	Kali 192.168.1.113	Windows 192.168.1.111	Filtered
Trusted ICMP traffic	Snort 192.168.1.110	Windows 192.168.1.111	Allowed
Trusted ICMP traffic	pfSense 192.168.1.1	Windows 192.168.1.111	Allowed

The results demonstrate that Windows Defender Firewall was capable of differentiating between trusted and unauthorised sources and enforcing the configured access-control policy accordingly.
Discussion
The findings demonstrate that Windows Defender Firewall provides an effective endpoint-level enforcement layer within the proposed defence-in-depth architecture. Its ability to restrict Kali while permitting communication from the trusted Snort and pfSense infrastructure reduces the exposure of Windows services to unauthorised hosts.
However, the experiment also demonstrates the limitation of relying on endpoint protection alone. Windows Defender Firewall primarily provides local enforcement at the protected endpoint; it does not provide the same network-wide detection capability as an IDS or the gateway-level filtering capability provided by pfSense. This reinforces the need for complementary controls.
Consequently, the Windows endpoint protection results support the research proposition that endpoint firewall protection is an important but not sufficient component of enterprise defence-in-depth. When combined with Snort IDS for detection and pfSense UTM for gateway-level traffic enforcement, the endpoint firewall contributes to a broader layered security architecture in which different controls provide protection at different points in the network.
5.4	Snot IDS Detection Results
The Snort IDS experiment evaluated the ability of Snort to detect and generate alerts for predefined suspicious network activity within the laboratory environment. The test used Kali Linux (192.168.1.113) as the controlled testing host and the Windows endpoint (192.168.1.111) as the destination. The Snort IDS host was configured on the enp0s8 interface with IP address 192.168.1.110.

Findings
The initial test demonstrated that network packet visibility and intrusion detection are separate functions. During the experiment, tcpdump confirmed that ICMP traffic generated by Kali was visible on the Snort monitoring interface. However, Snort initially generated no alert because an appropriate detection signature for the test traffic had not yet been configured.
A dedicated Snort rule was subsequently created to identify ICMP activity originating from Kali and directed towards the Windows endpoint:
alert icmp 192.168.1.113 any -> 192.168.1.111 any (msg:"SCENARIO 5 - ICMP activity from Kali to Windows"; sid:1000001; rev:1;)
The rule defined the Kali host as the source and the Windows endpoint as the destination, allowing the experiment to specifically identify the controlled suspicious activity generated for Scenario 5.
Following deployment of the rule, the test was repeated using:
ping -c 4 192.168.1.111
from the Kali Linux host.
Snort successfully generated four IDS alerts, corresponding to the four ICMP Echo Requests generated by the ping command. The alerts contained the following information:
[1:1000001:1] "SCENARIO 5 - ICMP activity from Kali to Windows" {ICMP} 192.168.1.113 -> 192.168.1.111

Results
Parameter	Result
Source	Kali Linux — 192.168.1.113
Destination	Windows — 192.168.1.111
Protocol	ICMP
Test traffic	4 ICMP Echo Requests
Snort interface	enp0s8
Snort IP	192.168.1.110
Signature ID	1000001
Alerts generated	4
Detection result	Successful

The one-to-one relationship between the four ICMP requests generated by Kali and the four alerts generated by Snort provides clear evidence that the configured IDS rule successfully matched the test traffic.
Discussion
The results demonstrate that Snort can provide a network-level detection and alerting capability that is complementary to endpoint firewall enforcement. While Windows Defender Firewall was responsible for enforcing the access-control policy at the Windows endpoint, Snort independently observed the network activity and generated security alerts.
An important finding was that Snort did not automatically classify the initial ICMP traffic as suspicious simply because the packets were visible on the monitoring interface. The traffic became detectable for the purposes of the experiment after an appropriate signature was configured. This demonstrates the importance of correct rule configuration and signature selection when deploying an IDS.
The experiment also highlighted the importance of IDS placement and packet visibility. Before successful alert generation, the Snort interface had to receive the relevant traffic. Once the interface was able to observe the ICMP packets and the appropriate detection rule was loaded, Snort successfully generated alerts identifying the source, destination, protocol and signature associated with the activity.
Therefore, the Scenario 5 results demonstrate that Snort provides an effective detection and visibility layer within the proposed defence-in-depth architecture. It does not replace the Windows firewall's enforcement function; instead, it provides an additional capability by identifying and reporting suspicious network activity. This distinction is important because an endpoint control may block an unauthorised connection while an IDS simultaneously provides visibility into the attempted activity, allowing the two controls to operate as complementary layers of defence.
5.5 pfSense UTM Filtering and Blocking Results
The pfSense UTM experiment evaluated the ability of pfSense to enforce network-level traffic filtering and blocking policies on traffic traversing the network gateway. The experiment used Kali Linux (192.168.1.113) as the controlled testing host and pfSense (192.168.1.1) as the default gateway.
The experiment specifically evaluated whether pfSense could prevent Kali from accessing HTTPS services by applying a policy-based rule targeting TCP port 443.
Findings
A baseline connectivity test was first performed to establish that Kali could access HTTPS services through the pfSense gateway. This established that TCP/443 traffic was initially permitted and provided a control condition against which the blocking test could be evaluated.
A firewall rule was then configured on the pfSense LAN interface with the following parameters:

Parameter	Configuration
Action	Block
Source	192.168.1.113 — Kali Linux
Destination	Any
Protocol	TCP
Destination Port	443
Security Function	HTTPS traffic filtering
The rule was positioned appropriately within the firewall rule set and applied to the pfSense configuration.
Following application of the rule, Kali was prevented from establishing HTTPS connections through the gateway. The HTTPS connection test failed, demonstrating that pfSense successfully enforced the configured traffic policy.
The firewall state was subsequently restored by disabling the blocking rule and clearing the relevant firewall state. HTTPS connectivity from Kali was successfully re-established. This restoration provided additional evidence that the observed loss of connectivity was directly associated with the pfSense firewall policy rather than a permanent network or endpoint failure.
Results
The experimental results can be summarised as follows:
Test condition	Kali → HTTPS	Result
Baseline, before blocking rule	TCP/443 permitted	Successful
pfSense TCP/443 blocking rule enabled	TCP/443 blocked	Blocked
Blocking rule disabled	TCP/443 permitted	Successful
The experiment therefore produced the expected allow - block - restore sequence.

Discussion
The findings demonstrate that pfSense successfully provided gateway-level traffic enforcement. Unlike the Windows endpoint firewall experiment, which enforced a security policy directly on the protected Windows system, pfSense enforced the policy at the network gateway for traffic destined beyond the local network.
The use of a source-specific rule demonstrated that pfSense could apply granular access-control policies based on the originating host, transport protocol and destination port. In this experiment, traffic originating from Kali (192.168.1.113) and destined for TCP port 443 was denied while the blocking rule was active.
The successful restoration of HTTPS connectivity after disabling the rule is particularly significant because it provided a controlled comparison between the permitted and denied states. This reduces the likelihood that the observed connectivity failure resulted from an unrelated network condition.
The experiment also reinforces an important architectural distinction identified during the research. pfSense can enforce policies only on traffic that actually traverses the gateway. Because Kali and the Windows endpoint reside on the same 192.168.1.0/24 local network, direct Kali-to-Windows traffic does not necessarily pass through pfSense. The Scenario 6 experiment therefore deliberately used gateway-bound HTTPS traffic to evaluate pfSense's filtering capability.
Overall, the results demonstrate that pfSense provides an effective network-level enforcement layer within the proposed defence-in-depth architecture. Its ability to block Kali's HTTPS traffic at the gateway complements the endpoint-level protection provided by Windows Defender Firewall and the detection capability provided by Snort IDS. The combined findings demonstrate that different security controls can enforce or detect traffic at different points within the network, thereby increasing the overall resilience of the architecture.

5.6 Comparative Evaluation of IDS and UTM
The practical evaluation demonstrated that Snort IDS and pfSense UTM provide different but complementary security capabilities within the proposed Windows enterprise defence-in-depth architecture. Although both technologies contribute to network security, their primary functions, enforcement points and responses to suspicious traffic differ significantly.
Snort was evaluated primarily as a network intrusion detection mechanism, while pfSense was evaluated as a network-level traffic filtering and enforcement mechanism. The experimental results demonstrate that the two technologies should not be considered substitutes for one another. Instead, their effectiveness is enhanced when they are deployed as complementary security layers.
Comparative Findings
Security Capability	Snort IDS	pfSense UTM
Primary function	Detection and alerting	Traffic filtering and blocking
Security position	Network monitoring point	Network gateway
Traffic visibility	High, where traffic is visible to the monitoring interface	Traffic traversing the gateway
Intrusion detection	Yes	Limited/UTM-dependent
Alert generation	Yes	Firewall events/logs
Policy-based blocking	Primarily detection	Yes
Source-based filtering	Detection rule	Yes
Protocol-based control	Detection rule	Yes
Destination-port filtering	Detection rule	Yes
Endpoint protection	No	Indirect
Gateway enforcement	No	Yes
Response to Scenario 5 ICMP activity	Generated IDS alerts	Not the primary control evaluated
Response to Scenario 6 TCP/443 traffic	Not the primary enforcement mechanism	Blocked successfully
Snort IDS Findings
The Scenario 5 experiment demonstrated that Snort successfully detected the predefined ICMP activity generated by Kali Linux. Four ICMP Echo Requests generated from 192.168.1.113 towards 192.168.1.111 resulted in four corresponding Snort alerts.
This demonstrates Snort's principal strength as a visibility and detection layer. Snort was able to identify the traffic based on the configured signature and provide information about the source, destination and protocol associated with the detected activity.
However, the experiment also demonstrated that Snort's primary function in this architecture is detection rather than enforcement. The IDS identified and reported the activity but was not responsible for blocking the traffic at the network gateway.
pfSense UTM Findings
The Scenario 6 experiment demonstrated the complementary function of pfSense. A policy was configured to block TCP traffic destined for port 443 from the Kali Linux host (192.168.1.113). When the rule was active, Kali was prevented from establishing HTTPS connections through the pfSense gateway.
When the rule was disabled and the relevant firewall state was cleared, HTTPS connectivity was successfully restored.
This demonstrated that pfSense can act as an active enforcement point, applying granular policies based on source address, protocol and destination port.
Comparative Discussion
The practical results demonstrate a fundamental distinction between detection and prevention:
Snort identifies and reports suspicious activity, whereas pfSense can actively enforce a policy to permit or deny traffic.
This distinction is important within a defence-in-depth architecture. If only Snort were deployed, suspicious traffic could be identified but the IDS would not, by itself, provide the same gateway-level blocking function demonstrated by pfSense. Conversely, relying exclusively on pfSense would provide traffic enforcement but would not provide the same signature-based detection and alerting capability demonstrated by Snort.

The two technologies therefore address different security requirements:

