The experiments also revealed that security effectiveness depends on correct placement. Snort must be positioned where relevant traffic can be observed, while pfSense can enforce policies only on traffic that traverses its gateway. This distinction was particularly important because the Kali and Windows systems were located on the same local subnet. Consequently, direct Kali-to-Windows traffic did not necessarily pass through pfSense, whereas gateway-bound HTTPS traffic provided an appropriate path for evaluating pfSense's filtering capability.

Overall Comparative Result
The results demonstrate that Snort IDS and pfSense UTM are complementary rather than competing technologies. Snort contributes network visibility, detection and alerting, while pfSense contributes policy-based traffic enforcement at the gateway.
When combined with Windows Defender Firewall at the endpoint, the three controls provide different defensive functions:
Security Layer	Technology	Primary Function
Endpoint	Windows Defender Firewall	Prevent unauthorised access
Network detection	Snort IDS	Detect and alert on suspicious activity
Network gateway	pfSense UTM	Filter and block gateway-bound traffic

Therefore, the comparative evaluation supports the central premise of the research: effective enterprise defence-in-depth is achieved not by selecting a single "best" security technology, but by combining complementary controls that provide prevention, detection and enforcement at appropriate points within the network architecture.
5.7 Defence-in-Depth Effectiveness
The practical evaluation demonstrated that the proposed defence-in-depth architecture provides complementary layers of protection across the Windows enterprise environment. Rather than relying on a single security mechanism, the architecture combined endpoint protection through Windows Defender Firewall, network-based detection through Snort IDS, and gateway-level traffic enforcement through pfSense UTM.
The results from the individual experiments demonstrate that each security control performed a distinct security function.

Layer 1 — Endpoint Protection
The Windows Defender Firewall experiment demonstrated that the Windows endpoint could enforce access-control policies independently of the other security controls. Traffic originating from the unauthorised Kali Linux host (192.168.1.113) was restricted, while trusted infrastructure such as the pfSense gateway (192.168.1.1) and Snort host (192.168.1.110) was permitted.
The reconnaissance experiment further demonstrated the effectiveness of this control. Although Microsoft networking services such as TCP ports 135, 139 and 445 may provide legitimate enterprise functionality, unauthorised access to these services was restricted by the endpoint firewall, with the tested ports reported as filtered.
This demonstrates the value of maintaining protection directly on the endpoint. Even where an unauthorised host is already present on the local network, the endpoint can independently enforce its own security policy.
Layer 2 — Intrusion Detection
Snort provided a separate detection layer. The Scenario 5 experiment demonstrated that Snort successfully identified the predefined ICMP activity generated by Kali and generated four corresponding alerts for four ICMP Echo Requests.
The result demonstrates that the IDS provides a capability that is different from firewall enforcement. Windows Defender Firewall can prevent unauthorised communication, while Snort provides visibility into network activity and generates security alerts when traffic matches a configured detection signature.
This additional visibility is important because prevention and detection serve different security objectives. A firewall may prevent an attempted connection, but an IDS can provide evidence that the attempt occurred.

Layer 3 — Gateway-Level Enforcement
The pfSense experiment provided the third major defensive layer. A policy was configured to prevent Kali (192.168.1.113) from accessing HTTPS services using TCP port 443 through the gateway.
The experiment successfully demonstrated the sequence:
Allow → Block → Restore
HTTPS connectivity was initially available, was subsequently blocked when the pfSense rule was enabled, and was restored after the rule was disabled and the relevant firewall state was cleared.
This demonstrates that pfSense can provide centralised, policy-based enforcement for traffic that traverses the network gateway.
Combined Defence-in-Depth Effect
The results demonstrate that the three security controls address different points within the attack and defence process:

