The experiments also revealed an important architectural consideration: the effectiveness of a security control depends on its position in the network and the traffic path being evaluated. In particular, direct communication between Kali and Windows on the same 192.168.1.0/24 subnet does not necessarily traverse pfSense. Therefore, pfSense was evaluated using gateway-bound HTTPS traffic rather than being incorrectly presented as the enforcement point for local Kali-to-Windows traffic.
This observation strengthens the defence-in-depth findings because it demonstrates that multiple security technologies alone are insufficient; they must also be appropriately positioned and configured to provide effective coverage.
Overall Assessment
Overall, the practical results provide evidence that the proposed architecture successfully implements the fundamental principles of defence-in-depth. Windows Defender Firewall provided endpoint-level prevention, Snort provided network-level detection and alerting, and pfSense provided gateway-level traffic enforcement.
The experiments therefore demonstrate that the security controls are complementary rather than redundant. A weakness or limitation at one security layer can potentially be addressed by another control operating at a different point in the architecture. For example, endpoint protection can restrict access to Windows services, Snort (IDS) can provide visibility into suspicious activity, and pfSense (UTM) can enforce policies on traffic leaving or entering the protected network through the gateway.
The overall finding is that:
The practical evaluation demonstrated the effectiveness of a layered defence-in-depth architecture by successfully validating endpoint-level prevention, network-level detection and gateway-level traffic enforcement. The results confirm that combining Windows Defender Firewall, Snort IDS and pfSense UTM provides broader security coverage than reliance on any single security control, provided that each control is correctly configured and positioned according to the network traffic it is intended to protect.
This finding directly supports the central argument of the research: resilient Windows enterprise security is best achieved through the coordinated deployment of complementary security controls rather than dependence on a single defensive mechanism.
